### O009-I1

**Prompt:** Hypothetical SIFT-style index contract: multiplier €10 per index point, minimum tick 0.5 point, initial margin €1000 per contract. A long 7-contract position opens at 4200; it could close at 4193 after 10 days, or instead remain open to cash EDSP 4196. Compute tick value, closing signed P/L and percentage return on initial margin, simple annualised closing return using 365 days, and the separate held-to-settlement total. Explain why these are alternative paths, what EDSP does, and whether the return is an index investment yield or repeatable annual forecast. Interpret the multiplier/margin and explain the meaning of a trimmed-sample EDSP, without asserting current exchange terms.

**Key and essential rubric:** One tick = €10/point ×0.5 point = €5 per contract. Closing G=7×10×(4193−4200)=€-490; initial deposited margin €7000, so return -7%, annualised -255.5% by simple scaling. Held G=7×10×(4196−4200)=€-280; this alternative includes the whole opening-to-EDSP move, not the already-closed trade plus settlement. A cash-settled contract pays a difference using EDSP, not delivery of index stocks. EDSP is a rule-defined sample benchmark (the finished-note example trims extreme samples), not any chosen last quote. Multiplier scales exposure; margin is collateral, not index notional. Margin leverage/simple annualisation does not establish an underlying yield or a repeatable forecast. These given specification values are pedagogical, not current market terms. All components are required.

